model {
  extend basalt.modelGateway {
    metadata {
      constraint "The runtime adapter replaces the single pool controller instance every few hours whatever the load, and cuts every request in flight at that instant; a replacement must not fail an agent's turn. The model gateway entry treats the platform's reset text or its retryable flag, never an overloaded one, as a controller reset, and resolves the controller again for each send because a stub that threw this error fails again at once. Only an explicit allow-list of model routes is replayable, where a repeat costs at most the tokens of one response: messages, responses, chat and plain completions, embeddings, models and generate-content; a new route is not replayed until it is added. A route that starts a job or makes a paid artifact, such as images, video or speech, is never sent twice, because the provider may already have accepted it. Before the first response byte, a safe read (GET or HEAD without an upgrade, excluding video status polls) or an allow-listed model call without an upgrade is sent once more after one second. A model call's body is read from a tee beside the real send and held, up to 4 MiB, only until the controller's final answer or the 20-second replay window closes; a larger body is sent once, and the copy giving up mid-body never changes the real send. Held copies in one isolate share a 32 MiB budget, and each reservation expires with its own window even when its request never cleans up. Past the budget a call is forwarded unheld and is not replayable, and one POOL_CONTROLLER_HOLD_SKIPPED line records count 1, request identifier, endpoint template and held bytes. A call whose first send ran past 20 seconds is not sent again, because a device call's signed assertion lives 25 seconds. The pool records each attempt on its own, except an unsent replay, which carries the refused attempt's identifier. A job-starting call cut before its answer gets the cautious unknown-outcome answer, so its client decides. A larger body, a late or second reset or a model socket gets the native retry advice: status 503 with an api error for messages and a server error for other model routes, with Retry-After of one second. It is never an overload answer, because the messages client gives up after three of those. A control write keeps its existing failure, because its callers may not assume it was not applied. A started stream is never replayed; a messages or responses event stream ends with the native retry terminal, an api error or a failed response, which the model clients retry by themselves, and a reader that leaves is not an interruption. Other started bodies and model sockets are still cut at this hop and are not recorded there. The shared answer for a model call whose outcome is unknown after dispatch is 502; on an allow-listed route it carries Retry-After of one second and no longer asks the client not to retry, and on every other route, or when the caller names no route, it keeps the cautious do-not-retry answer. Tool calls keep their not-replayed answer. Every failed controller call writes one POOL_CONTROLLER_CALL_FAILED line with the request identifier, endpoint template, attempt, elapsed time, closed message class and the platform's retryable, overloaded, remote and reset flags. Each event writes one POOL_CONTROLLER_INTERRUPTED line with count 1, outcome, saved (a replay the controller answered successfully), reason, cause, method and endpoint template, and, unless it is a saved unsent replay, one pool_controller_interrupted observability adapter event joined by request identifier. Exception text, query strings and bodies never enter either. When the controller answers 503 with its unsent mark, which states that no byte of the call reached a runtime and names that attempt's identifier, the model gateway entry sends an allow-listed call it still holds once more after one second. The replay carries the attempt identifier, so the controller records both attempts as one request. The same hold, replay window and allow-list apply, and a call is replayed at most once, whichever answer triggered it. A call whose body is not held gets the native retry advice under the attempt identifier; a model socket, a read or a route off the allow-list keeps the controller's own answer. The entry removes the mark from every answer except a socket answer, which the controller strips, and the attempt identifier from every caller request, so a client never sees either and cannot choose the identifier. Each unsent first answer writes one POOL_CONTROLLER_INTERRUPTED line with cause unsent under the attempt identifier, including when its replay fails; that call still gets the native retry advice unless its client left. A failed replay's controller call line carries the same identifier."
    }
  }
}
